WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
A graph says these ten features do it. Do they?. circuit-tracer ranks features by influence on one prompt. In two studies the top ten turned out to be readouts: switch them off and the behaviour stays. circuitcheck tests that, compares graphs across training, and labels features with a trust score.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Install from a clone (PyPI: pip install circuitcheck, once released). No torch needed.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.976
The validator: is a group of graph features a switch or a readout?

4
00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:30.952
A toy model with known answers: one switch, two readouts, a backup pair.

5
00:00:30.952 --> 00:00:38.928
Ablation, steering and a counterfactual patch, each against 20 random

6
00:00:38.928 --> 00:00:46.904
and 20 matched-norm control sets. The verdicts match the ground truth.

7
00:00:46.904 --> 00:00:54.880
A readout: big activation, but switching it off moves nothing beyond controls.

8
00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.980
The validator's report: one row per group of features, with its verdict.

9
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.480
Grey ticks: what ablating random size-matched features does. Orange: the group itself.

10
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.480
Every verdict lists its numbers, and the rules are printed with the report.

11
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:16.570
The graph diff: what did RL change inside Qwen3-1.7B?

12
00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:24.860
48 attribution graphs: 8 prompts, before and after RL, three training runs.

13
00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:33.150
The same ten features the study picked by hand, in the same order.

14
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:41.440
validity: ok -> RL did not make the base-model transcoders fit worse (FVU 0.500 vs 0.499).

15
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.540
The diff report: features gained, lost and reweighted by RL, against a swap null.

16
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.040
The top ten features, each checked against a control run that did not learn to hack.

17
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.040
Stability: two random halves of the prompts agree on 70% of the top ten (chance: 0.2%).

18
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.540
Validity: the transcoders were trained on the base model, so reconstruction error is checked.

19
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:07.467
Feature labels: Claude Haiku 5.5 explains each feature from its top examples,

20
00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:15.893
then a detector scores the label on held-out examples it never saw.

21
00:02:15.893 --> 00:02:24.320
Every reply is cached: this rerun made 0 API calls. The full 50-feature run cost $0.26.

22
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.920
The labels report: each feature's label, the explainer's confidence and the held-out scores.

23
00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.920
Calibration: how well stated confidence and detector probabilities match what happened.

24
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.440
Test the claim before you publish it. validate: switch or readout, against controls. diff: what training changed, against a null. label: cheap labels with held-out scores. github.com/albertlieyingadrian/circuitcheck
